blocks.story:
Patrika's Playwright suite was 184 specs. Roughly 60% used CSS selectors lifted straight from the component code: `await page.click('.btn.btn-primary.rounded-lg.px-4.py-2')`. It worked. CI was green. Everyone was happy.

On January 9, 2025, the design system team upgraded Tailwind from v3.3 to v3.4. The upgrade renamed exactly **one** utility class chain — the default button preset reorganized its modifiers, so `.btn-primary` became `.btn-primary.shadow-sm`, and `rounded-lg` became `rounded-md` per the new design tokens.

The next CI run had **71 red specs**. Every locator that depended on the old Tailwind class chain broke. None of them were testing styling — they were testing *behavior* — but the selectors had been written against implementation details that the upgrade quietly changed.

It took two engineers a full day (≈14 hours combined) to fix the locators by hand. The fix was rewriting them all to use Playwright's role-based and label-based locators:

```ts
// Before (brittle)
await page.click('.btn.btn-primary.rounded-lg.px-4.py-2');

// After (durable)
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Publish article' }).click();
```

Over the next 6 months Patrika upgraded Tailwind twice more (3.4 → 3.5 → 4.0-beta). The new locators survived both upgrades with **zero changes**. The team estimates they saved ~28 engineering-hours and prevented two skipped releases.

